templ kafkaBasicUsage() {
	@docLead() {
		A producer needs the broker's address and a topic; a consumer needs the address, a topic, and a group. These are the same lines the kafka/producer-consumer starter runs, using the franz-go client.
	}
	@docH2("connect", "Connecting")
	@docP() {
		Breadboard puts the broker's address in @docCodeWord("KAFKA_BROKERS") and points @docCodeWord("SSL_CERT_FILE") at its CA bundle, so the client needs no addresses or certificate paths of its own. What does have to be in code is opting into TLS: a Kafka client connects in plaintext unless told otherwise.
	}
	@docCode("cmd/producer/main.go", kafkaConnectCode)
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("kgo.NewClient") does not connect yet. It contacts a seed broker on first use, asks it for the cluster's metadata (which brokers lead which partitions), and from then on talks to each partition's leader directly.
	}
	@docH2("produce", "Producing a record")
	@docCode("cmd/producer/main.go", kafkaProduceCode)
	@docP() {
		What actually happens: the client hashes the key to choose a partition, adds the record to that partition's in-memory batch, and sends the batch to the partition's leader. The leader appends it to the partition's log file and replies with the offset it was given. @docCodeWord("ProduceSync") waits for that reply; @docCodeWord("Produce") takes a callback instead, so many records can be in flight at once.
	}
	@docH2("consume", "Consuming records")
	@docCode("cmd/consumer/main.go", kafkaConsumeCode)
	@docP() {
		Each @docCodeWord("PollFetches") returns whatever records arrived since the last call, from every partition this consumer owns. The consumer only reads; nothing is removed from the topic. Its position is an offset per partition, which it commits back to Kafka so a restart resumes where it left off (see @docLink("/docs/kafka/offsets-and-delivery", "Offsets and delivery")).
	}
	@docCallout("Why ConsumeResetOffset") {
		franz-go splits initial reads from error recovery: @docCodeWord("ConsumeStartOffset") sets where a brand-new group with no committed offsets starts (its default is already @docCodeWord("AtStart()"), the oldest record), while @docCodeWord("ConsumeResetOffset") sets where a consumer resumes if its offset falls out of range or a broker loses data (its default is @docCodeWord("RewindOffset(time.Minute)"), one minute back in time). Passing @docCodeWord("ConsumeResetOffset(kgo.NewOffset().AtStart())") makes both initial reads and out-of-range resets start from the oldest record.
	}
	@docH2("run", "Run it")
	@docTerminal("bb init kafka-lab", "bb starter kafka/producer-consumer", "bb up", "bb logs consumer -f")
}

templ kafkaTopics() {
	@docLead() {
		A topic is a named log. A partition is one of the independent, ordered logs a topic is split into: the unit of parallelism, ordering and storage.
	}
	@docH2("topics", "Topics are logs")
	@docP() {
		Each partition is an append-only sequence of records numbered from 0. That number is the offset. Records stay for the topic's retention period (seven days by default), whether or not they were read, so any number of consumers can read the same partition independently.
	}
	@docH2("creating", "Creating a topic")
	@docP() {
		The broker in Breadboard does not create topics automatically, so code asks for one explicitly with the admin client. The partition count is the key decision: it caps how many consumers in one group can work in parallel.
	}
	@docCode("cmd/producer/main.go", kafkaCreateTopicCode)
	@docP() {
		The replication factor is 1 because the lab has one broker. In production it is usually 3, so each partition has a leader and two followers that take over if the leader's broker fails.
	}
	@docH2("keys", "Keys pick the partition")
	@docP() {
		When a record has a key, franz-go hashes it with murmur2, exactly as the Java client does, and takes the result modulo the number of partitions. The same key therefore always lands on the same partition, while the partition count stays the same.
	}
	@docList(
		"Records with the key customer-3 always go to one partition, in the order they were produced.",
		"Records without a key are spread across partitions in batches, for throughput.",
		"Adding partitions later changes the modulo, so existing keys can move: plan the count up front.",
	)
	@docH2("order", "Ordering")
	@docP() {
		Kafka guarantees order within a partition, never across a topic. If two events must be processed in order (an order's created and paid events, say), give them the same key so they share a partition.
	}
	@docTerminal("bb logs producer", "# every record for one customer shows the same partition")
}

templ kafkaGroups() {
	@docLead() {
		A consumer group is a set of consumers that share the work of reading a topic. Kafka assigns each partition to exactly one member of the group, so partitions are the unit of parallelism: a group can never have more active members than the topic has partitions.
	}
	@docFigure("Two members split three partitions. Add a third member and each owns one; add a fourth and it sits idle.") {
		@kafkaGroupDiagram()
	}
	@docH2("joining", "Joining a group")
	@docP() {
		Membership is a client setting, not something you create on the broker. Any client that sets the same group ID joins the same group. With franz-go that is one option:
	}
	@docCode("cmd/consumer/main.go", kafkaGroupCode)
	@docP() {
		What actually happens when this client starts: it asks the brokers which one coordinates the group, sends that coordinator a JoinGroup request, and receives the list of members. One member, the leader, computes which partitions each member gets and sends the plan back with SyncGroup. Then every member fetches only its own partitions and sends a heartbeat every 3 seconds to stay in the group.
	}
	@docH2("assignment", "Who owns which partition")
	@docP() {
		franz-go's default assignment strategy is cooperative-sticky: partitions stay with their current owner where possible, and a rebalance only moves the partitions that have to move, while every other member keeps reading.
	}
	@docH2("rebalance", "What triggers a rebalance")
	@docList(
		"A member joins or leaves the group, including a crash or an OOM kill.",
		"A member misses heartbeats for longer than the session timeout (45 seconds by default in franz-go).",
		"The partition count of a subscribed topic changes.",
	)
	@docP() {
		While partitions move, their records are not being read, so lag grows briefly. A member that dies without leaving cleanly keeps its partitions until its session times out, which is why killing a consumer pauses its partitions for up to 45 seconds.
	}
	@docH2("try", "Try it in Breadboard")
	@docSteps(
		"Apply the kafka/producer-consumer starter and run bb up.",
		"Copy the consumer app in experiment.yaml to consumer-2 and run bb up again.",
		"Open the kafka service's Topics & groups tab: the group now has 2 members.",
		"Stop one with docker stop and watch the other take over its partitions after the session timeout.",
	)
}

