		<text x="426" y="113" class="fill-slate-900 text-[13px] font-semibold">consumer-2</text>
		<text x="594" y="113" text-anchor="end" class="fill-slate-500 text-[12px]">owns p2</text>
	</svg>
}

templ kafkaOffsets() {
	@docLead() {
		An offset is a consumer group's position in a partition. When it is committed, relative to when the work is done, decides whether a crash loses records or repeats them.
	}
	@docH2("offsets", "Committed offsets")
	@docP() {
		A group commits, per partition, the offset of the next record it wants. Kafka stores these commits in an internal topic, @docCodeWord("__consumer_offsets"), which you can see in the Topics & groups tab. Lag is the difference between a partition's end and the group's committed offset: records written but not yet processed.
	}
	@docH2("auto", "Auto-commit")
	@docP() {
		By default franz-go commits automatically every 5 seconds, for records already returned by @docCodeWord("PollFetches")&#46; If the consumer crashes after processing records but before the next commit, those records are delivered again after restart. That is at-least-once delivery, and it is why the kafka/producer-consumer starter's Break this section asks you to kill a consumer and count duplicates.
	}
	@docH2("manual", "Committing after the work")
	@docP() {
		The kafka/sink-to-postgres starter turns auto-commit off and commits only after its database writes succeed. A crash can then only cause a replay, never a lost record:
	}
	@docCode("cmd/sink/main.go", kafkaManualCommitCode)
	@docList(
		"Commit before the work: a crash loses records. That is at-most-once.",
		"Commit after the work: a crash repeats records. That is at-least-once.",
		"Exactly-once needs the work and the commit to succeed or fail together (Kafka transactions), or the work to be idempotent.",
	)
	@docH2("idempotent", "Making replays harmless")
	@docP() {
		The practical route to effectively-once is at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing. The sink gives every order a unique ID and inserts with a conflict clause, so a replayed order changes nothing:
	}
	@docCode("cmd/sink/main.go", kafkaIdempotentCode)
	@docTerminal("bb starter kafka/sink-to-postgres", "bb up", "docker kill bb-kafka-lab-sink-1 && bb up", "bb logs sink | grep duplicate")
}

templ kafkaInBreadboard() {
	@docLead() {
		What Breadboard runs when you add Kafka, and how it is wired.
	}
	@docH2("node", "One node, no ZooKeeper")
	@docP() {
		The service runs the official @docCodeWord("apache/kafka") image in KRaft mode: one process is both the broker that stores partitions and the controller that manages cluster metadata, so there is no ZooKeeper. With one node the replication factor is 1, and if the container stops every partition is unavailable until it comes back.
	}
	@docH2("tls", "TLS on 9093")
	@docP() {
		Clients connect to the SSL listener at @docCodeWord("kafka.<domain>:9093")&#46; The broker's certificate is issued by your Breadboard CA for that name, and clients verify it. There is no client authentication in v1. A plaintext listener on 9092 exists only when an app asks for one with @docCodeWord("tls.mode: off"), and only on a private network shared with that app.
	}
	@docH2("env", "What your app receives")
	@docList(
		"KAFKA_BROKERS: the broker address, kafka.<domain>:9093.",
		"KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL: SSL, for clients that read it.",
		"SSL_CERT_FILE: the trust bundle Go uses to verify the broker.",
	)
	@docH2("tools", "Using the Kafka CLI tools")
	@docP() {
		The image ships Kafka's own scripts, and Breadboard mounts a client configuration that already trusts the CA. This is what the dashboard's Topics & groups tab runs:
	}
	@docTerminal("docker exec -it bb-kafka-lab-kafka-1 /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server kafka.bb.internal:9093 --command-config /etc/bb/config/client.properties --describe")
}

// kafkaGroupsLive shows the consumer-groups concept in the reader's running
// experiment, or nothing when there is no Kafka to read.
templ kafkaGroupsLive(experiment, service string, k *kafkaLive) {
	<div class="my-6 rounded-xl border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50 px-5 py-4">
		<div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-800">
			@dot("blue")
			In { experiment } right now
		</div>
		<p class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-6 text-slate-700">{ k.Sentence }</p>
		<a href={ templ.SafeURL("/services/" + service + "?tab=explore") } class="mt-1 inline-block text-sm text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-800">Open { service } in the dashboard →</a>
	</div>
}
